User Flows Overview

This document outlines the core user journeys within the educational video platform. Each role interacts with the system differently and has access to different features based on permissions. These flows are essential to define application behavior, UI layout, and API endpoints.

1. User Flow (Registered User)

Role: User

Actions:

Sign up or log in

View dashboard with assigned courses and videos

View video categories and search/filter content

Watch videos and see preview/description

Track personal progress for each video/course

Read attached course documents

Leave comments on videos (if enabled)

Screens:

Login / Register

Dashboard

Video listing by category

Video detail (with YouTube embed, transcript, related videos)

Course detail (with video list and progress)

My Progress page

2. Admin Flow

Role: Admin

Actions:

Manage users and assign roles (Admin, Team Lead, User, Guest)

Add/edit/delete videos and courses

Assign videos or courses to users (individually or bulk)

Create and update categories, tags, and carousel order

Monitor user progress and access stats

Moderate comments

Screens:

Admin Dashboard

User Management

Video Management

Course Management

Assignment Panel

Analytics Panel

3. Team Lead Flow

Role: Team Lead

Actions:

Create new courses from available videos

Assign existing videos/courses to users

View team members' progress

Filter and search content

Screens:

Team Dashboard

Course Builder

Assignment Manager

Progress Overview

4. Guest Flow

Role: Guest

Actions:

Browse public videos and categories (limited)

View basic details and preview of some content

Sign up prompt on restricted actions (e.g. full watch, comments)

Screens:

Home (public)

Category Explorer (limited content)

Video preview modal or page

Sign up / Login CTA

Notes

All user journeys assume responsive UI and accessibility compliance.

Guest experience is designed to encourage registration.

All role permissions align with RBAC definitions in the backend structure document.

Summary

This flow specification will be used to design both the frontend components and backend APIs. Each action corresponds to at least one API endpoint, and the UI/UX will be designed around these flows for clarity and ease of use. 